Z||000000||Philphine||10-04-2001||06:53 PM||||i don't think my pedal is the problem but isn't that just for '84 only anyway?<P> i'm grasping at straws with my clutch action on my '87 coupe (izuzu 5 sp). it was shifting really hard about mid summer so i got a new master and slave and made sure to get gm ones (replacing parts i had just replaced about a year and a half ago, along with a new clutch), and bleed it till i pretty much had all new fluid and it worked good for about a month. now i'm back to grinding gears. i also noticed the fluid looks dirtier than i would have expected for so short a time.<P>i'm at a loss. my truck has an hydrualic clutch too and it's always worked well. the two fiero 4 speeds i've driven also worked a lot better (though i haven't driven either as long). i don't know what else to try beyond another shot at a complete bleed.i don't want to buy the parts this soon again. any help appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||LivoniaFieroGT||10-04-2001||08:43 PM||||You might try a slow bleed method that I used.  It works when you're the only one present.  On a slight incline, pop the cylinder cap for adding fluid.  Then with the slave at a lower plane than the cyinder, loosen the bleeder nut and run it out.  Keep filling the cylinder as you go so it doesn't run low.  Then after a few minutes, seal the bleeder nut secure.  Takes out any air bubbles and most all the dirty fluid.<BR>I had a problem where I was forced to bleed the line using this method after long drives.  Gears would be tougher to engage or crunch. Yet around town for weeks it never needed help.... go figure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||roadwarrior||10-04-2001||08:51 PM||||I also think I heard someone in here mention that it can have something to do with your tranny lube, it just seems that some fiero transmisions work better with diferent types of lube, and this make sense cause if a tanny's lube it too think the linkages will be harder to move as will the forks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||10-04-2001||09:49 PM||||the slow bleed is what worked for me this summer. i kept getting air when i forced it in any way. i guess this weekend i can set it up and let it bleed for a while. at this point what i'd like to do is put a 4speed in. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TooManyFieros||10-05-2001||10:14 AM||||Been here and done this. Can you check a few things for me? 1. When bled, do you have 1 1/4" throw at the slave? Have someone push the clutch pedal in all the way and you extend a tape measure out against the slave arm (down as close as you can to where the slave rod enters the arm.) Mark the tape with your thumb, and as they release the clutch pedal, let the tape slip through your fingers. This throw is very important.  2. no dirt - If you really bled this system as good as you think, you should have gotten all the dark stuff out. When you think you have blead it as well as you can, take the bleeder screw out and "slowly" pull the slave arm back to push the slave piston back into the cylinder. Usually there is much air still in there. Put the bleeder back in and tighten without releasing the arm. Now, it should work great. If not, measure the throw at the slave and see what you have.     Let me know what you find and we can go from here.  Jim||||reg||7
Z||000005||TooManyFieros||10-05-2001||10:16 AM||||Oh yea! When everything is bled, how close to the floor does the clutch grab? Like immediately, as soon as you start to release it?||||reg||1
